// A program is what it is written as. `**` reads one off a block handed to a
// rule, and what it reads is a program in turn — so what holds a program holds
// programs all the way down.
//
// The engine hands over two things and no more: the first statement, and what
// is left of the program after it. The chain that holds them is built in the
// language, with the same `chain` and `&=` as everything else, so a program is
// made of the language rather than of the engine.

mark ^keyword := external global

Written ^keyword := class {
  of (code) => code**
}
taken ^keyword := Written.of({
  one
  two
  three
})
unless (exists taken) { INFO@mark `PG1 a block handed to a rule is not read as a program` }
unless (exists taken.written) { INFO@mark `PG3 a program is not written as anything` }
unless (taken.count == three) { INFO@mark `PG8 a program of three statements does not count three` }
walked ^keyword := zero
taken for { walked = walked.succ }
unless (walked == three) { INFO@mark `PG9 walking a program of three does not visit three` }
unless (exists taken.first.value.written) { INFO@mark `PG5 a statement is not a program in turn` }
unless (taken.first.value.count == one) { INFO@mark `PG10 a statement is written as other than one thing` }

// A program of one statement is written as that one.
alone ^keyword := Written.of({ five })
unless (alone.count == one) { INFO@mark `PG11 a program of one statement counts other than one` }

// What it is read with: a level is a set of rules, and a program says which.
unless (exists taken.O) { INFO@mark `PG12 a program does not say which rules it is read with` }

// spec: running a program does not answer what it says — `p()` is not how a
// program is run, and neither is running one of the statements it is written
// as. What a program is *for* is being read back: `**` is asked for one at
// every method definition, and what runs is the method.
